
Syncytin-1 is expressed in placental tissues and mediates the fusion of trophoblast cells to form the syncytiotrophoblast, a specialised multinucleated layer that is essential for normal placental function. The protein retains the membrane-fusion activity characteristic of retroviral envelope proteins, providing a striking example of how host organisms can repurpose viral genes for their own benefit.

The discovery of Syncytin-1 demonstrated that endogenous retroviruses have contributed directly to the evolution of mammalian reproduction. Together with Syncytin-2 and other independently acquired syncytin genes, it illustrates how viral envelope proteins have repeatedly been recruited during the evolution of placental mammals.
